"""Source-art review and proposed character pipeline; no simulated 3D claims."""
from pathlib import Path
from html import escape as e
import json

def render_agents():
    root=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[3]
    packs={name:json.loads((root/f'apps/lab/lib/assets/generated/{name}.json').read_text())['assets'] for name in ['loopforge','loopforge-console','loopforge-supervisors']}
    def pic(pack,key,label):
        vs=packs[pack][key]['variants'];v=vs[0]
        sources=', '.join(f'{x["src"]} {x["width"]}w' for x in vs)
        return f'<figure><a href="{vs[-1]["src"]}" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="{v["src"]}" srcset="{sources}" sizes="(max-width:700px) 90vw, 520px" width="{v["width"]}" height="{v["height"]}" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="{e(label)}"></a><figcaption>{e(label)} · original concept reference, not a new 3D render</figcaption></figure>'
    cards=[
      ('Workers','thrum-conveyor','loopforge-supervisors','Worn metal, exposed joints, industrial faces. Smaller and less ornate than a supervisor.','Start with three compatible bodies. Weight transfers into a held brain cradle; hands meet the handles; feet plant at the workstation. Fatigue, hesitation and disorder need readable movement, not only badges.'),
      ('LIMEN','limen','loopforge','Heavy dark armour and one asymmetric cyan blade: recognize LIMEN in a dark CCTV feed before reading his name.','Plant a blocking stance at the gate. Look at a particular worker, stop them, inspect and release or hold. The queue behind them changes.'),
      ('STILETTO','stiletto-portrait','loopforge-console','Narrow black chrome, long segmented limbs, blade forearms and red visor.','Quick decisive turns and impatient checking. Workers hurry in response. Her shortcuts change clearance and exposure; they do not grant engineering ability.'),
      ('Cathexis','cathexis','loopforge','Cracked porcelain, warm eyes, crown/headphones, cable hair and distressed fabric.','Open hands and controlled attention. Workers turn toward her, ignore her or recoil according to their actual response. Influence should spread through bodies.'),
      ('Rivet Witch','witch','loopforge','Hood, green goggles, brass mask, tool belt, cables and heavy boots.','Kneel, brace, inspect, wrench and test at machine contacts. Repair motion only accompanies a real, eligible engineering task.'),
      ('Thrum','thrum','loopforge','Speaker head, blue lens, chest resonator and monk-like cloth.','Deliberate resonant movement. Bodies settle and tension releases; distraction and lost discipline can be visible costs.'),
    ]
    out='<header class="section-head"><span class="kicker">12 October · character direction</span><h2>A factory inhabited by agents.</h2><p>The world is prebuilt. The next visual investment is in its people: recognizable silhouettes, satisfying work and interactions that reveal what the factory is doing to them.</p></header><div class="callout">Agents because they have beliefs, desires and intentions. An LLM is optional. The production test proves an imported-rig/action path, but its bodies failed owner visual review. Main-play figures remain placeholders. The owner’s Meshy LIMEN is the current candidate. Its regenerated body and separate blade are assembled in the review below. Full CCTV identity acceptance and factory-contact motion remain open.</div><p><a href="AGENT_CHARACTER_AND_ANIMATION_DIRECTION.md">Full character and engine plan ↗</a> · <a href="/stepanoskin/loopforge/play">Play the prebuilt opening ↗</a></p><div class="callout"><a href="/stepanoskin/loopforge/play/agent-test">Open the agent production test →</a> · <a href="AGENT_PRODUCTION_TEST.md">Implementation and evidence ↗</a><p>Inspect clearance, cradle transfer and supervisor intervention. Switch cameras, scrub confirmed ticks, compare original art and benchmark 10 / 24 / 100 workers.</p></div><div class="callout"><a href="/stepanoskin/loopforge/play/agent-test/limen">Inspect LIMEN v2 and the animated blade →</a><p>42,354 body triangles, 23 Mixamo joints, a 4,994-triangle housing and procedural cyan core/braids. Turn, scrub, preview an energy surge and compare CCTV-scale angles in Security or the Lattice Forge. The 5.49 MB runtime package is temporarily hosted with the app while CloudFront is pending.</p><a href="production/LIMEN_MESHY_REVIEW.md">Asset provenance and review limits ↗</a></div><h3>Read the original cast.</h3><p>Open a reference at full size. Preserve body shape, material and gesture before adding fine scratches or more glow.</p><div class="agent-gallery">'
    blade = """<h3>Know LIMEN before you read his name.</h3>
    <div class="callout"><strong>The blade is how you tell LIMEN from other robots in a dark factory room through a CCTV camera angle.</strong><p>A heavy dark body, one asymmetric white-cyan edge and torn cyan braids. Shape, placement and motion carry identity together; colour or a name badge alone cannot do the job.</p></div>
    <div class="scale-lenses"><article><span class="kicker">AT REST</span><h4>A contained edge.</h4><p>A narrow luminous core remains readable. Braids crawl unevenly around it; the housing and LIMEN’s stance stay heavy and controlled.</p></article><article><span class="kicker">IN ACTION</span><h4>Gather. Lash. Decay.</h4><p>Energy tightens before intervention, releases brief strands and sparks at the visible action phase, then settles. No permanent fireworks.</p></article><article><span class="kicker">ON CCTV</span><h4>Identity survives distance.</h4><p>Keep the asymmetric outline and contrast. Drop tiny sparks and extra light first. Respect walls, body occlusion, reduced motion and sound-off play.</p></article></div>
    <details><summary>How to build it without overwhelming the room</summary><table class="pairs"><tbody>
    <tr><th scope="row">Housing</th><td>Prepare a roughly 2–5k-triangle textured mesh from the 1.15-million-triangle master; calibrate a rigid forearm socket and carrying pose.</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Living energy</th><td>Under 1k triangles for a clean core and a few shader-driven ribbons. Small shared noise data; no cloth or fluid simulation.</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Bursts and light</th><td>Start with 4–12 sparks per burst and a 24-spark room cap. Reuse existing bloom; add scoped light only if it earns its cost. All budgets are proposed, not measured results.</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Truth</th><td>Permitted action timing drives the phrase; cosmetic randomness stays separate. Never reveal private loyalty or confidence through glow, invent contact, or duplicate bursts when cameras change.</td></tr></tbody></table></details>
    <h3>First assembly ready for review; action effects come next.</h3>
    <p>The 42,354-triangle body, 23-joint rig and 4.32 MB review copy are available. The separate blade master and source record are verified in private S3. Babylon, the loader, factory lighting and bloom already exist. The live review now assembles v2 with a 4,994-triangle rigid housing and a 672-triangle procedural core/braids. Both GLBs total 5.49 MB.</p>
    <p>The revision-specific importer separates and simplifies the housing, retains reduced source maps and generates tangents. The calibrated socket passes sampled walk/run leg checks. Preview energy surges and three CCTV-scale framing presets are available. Next: author contact poses, action-linked sparks/trails and validate the complete crowded room. No new Meshy export is needed to start. The owner can generate a housing-only revision if inspection exposes a shape, mount or simplification problem; moving energy stays procedural. CloudFront is not a blocker for local or already-authorized temporary app-hosted review.</p>
    <div class="callout"><strong>The gate:</strong> hide labels and portraits, then identify LIMEN at actual feed size in all three dark room angles, beside workers and cyan machinery. His blade must remain readable without hiding their actions. Compare the same room with effects on/off at 10 / 24 / 100 workers; count all camera passes, frame-time tails and repeated-load memory.</div>
    <p><a href="LIMEN_BLADE_DESIGN.md">Full blade contract, readiness and implementation checklist ↗</a> · <a href="/stepanoskin/loopforge/architecture/webview">Rendering and engine boundary →</a></p>"""
    out=out.replace('<h3>Read the original cast.</h3>', blade+'<h3>Read the original cast.</h3>')
    for name,key,pack,shape,motion in cards:
        out+='<article>'+pic(pack,key,name+' / character and factory reference')+'<div><span class="kicker">'+e(name)+'</span><h3>'+e(shape)+'</h3><p>'+e(motion)+'</p></div></article>'
    out+='</div><h3>Replace the bodies; keep the action pipeline.</h3><p>The rejected test builds too much of the body from balls and capsules, then adds a shared noisy metal surface. The original art has fitted armour, tapered limbs, recessed mechanisms and wear tied to use.</p><table class="pairs"><tbody><tr><th>Shape</th><td>Author coherent head, torso, pelvis and limb shells in Blender. Curves belong to designed plates and bearings, not a repeated spherical body kit.</td></tr><tr><th>Surface</th><td>Deliberate UVs and baked detail; separate paint, bare metal and rubber responses. Edge wear, recess grime and polished contact surfaces follow construction.</td></tr><tr><th>Motion</th><td>Check real joint clearances, rigid armour, planted feet and cradle grips. Keep confirmed action timing and character identity across cameras.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Current gate: inspect the Meshy LIMEN in neutral grey, textured under factory lighting and using its supplied movement clips. Then author its actual gate interaction. Worker variants and the remaining supervisors still need their own shape and motion gates. Image-to-3D can supply a candidate mesh; cleanup and real-model review still decide whether it is usable.</p><p><a href="AGENT_CHARACTER_AND_ANIMATION_DIRECTION.md#model-direction-reset-after-production-review">Revised authoring routes and acceptance sequence ↗</a></p><h3>One encounter must work from beginning to end.</h3><div class="principle-grid">'
    for label,title,text in [('01 / APPROACH','A worker reaches the gate.','Reserve a real queue slot. Walk to it, slow down, stop and face LIMEN. No feet sliding through a closed gate.'),('02 / CONTACT','LIMEN inspects this worker.','Reserve both actors and the scanner. The credential, hands and device align. A refusal or interruption changes the action.'),('03 / CONSEQUENCE','Release or hold.','The simulation commits the result. Animation and sound express it. The worker moves; the waiting queue and later throughput respond.')]:
        out+=f'<article class="principle"><span class="kicker">{label}</span><h3>{title}</h3><p>{text}</p></article>'
    out+='</div><h3>The same characters; different costs to render.</h3><table class="pairs"><tbody>'
    for label,text in [('Close acting','Authored GLB rigs, clip blends, look targets and limited hand/foot correction for supervisors and close worker interactions.'),('Routine background work','Shared worker meshes/materials. Benchmark baked bone animation for distant repeated tasks; stock Babylon BVA cannot blend actions.'),('The room','Prebuilt geometry enables authored approach/contact points and baked static lighting. A few local dynamic shadows preserve depth.'),('Three feeds','One scene and pose update, three camera passes; one active feed on phones. Count the entire composition, not one camera in isolation.'),('Truth','BDI/action systems own paths, reservations, outcomes and history. The renderer receives permitted action timing and never changes production or trust.')]:out+=f'<tr><th>{label}</th><td>{text}</td></tr>'
    out+='</tbody></table><h3>Production test / review gate</h3><p>One worker family, LIMEN and STILETTO. Prove a complete gate inspection, a cradle transfer and one supervisor–worker exchange under the real room lighting. Then test 10, 24 and 100 agents before expanding to the rest of the cast.</p><p>Camera changes, quality settings and animation detail must preserve the same agent, task and outcome. Inspect the private causal trace without exposing it to the player prematurely.</p><div class="callout">Production-test implementation: offline-authored GLB rigs, named clips, paired reservations, cradle ownership, hand targets and a skeletal/baked comparison. This is a bounded author fixture; complete BDI planning, first-turn integration and physical-device certification remain open.</div><p><a href="https://doc.babylonjs.com/features/featuresDeepDive/animation/baked_texture_animations/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Baked-animation capabilities and limits ↗</a> · <a href="https://doc.babylonjs.com/features/featuresDeepDive/mesh/copies/thinInstances/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Instance visibility tradeoffs ↗</a></p>'
    return out
